22:48:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right to be surprised—and even frustrated! It *was* taught as fact in schools for decades, complete with neat diagrams and labels. So how could something so confidently presented in textbooks turn out to be completely wrong? It wasn’t “made up” out of nowhere, but rather it’s a classic case of how a small bit of real data got distorted and then fossilized in education long after science had moved on. Let me walk you through how that happens: 1. **A kernel of truth, blown out of proportion:** In 1901, D.P. Hanig published a study where he asked people to rate the intensity of sweet, sour, salty, and bitter tastes when drops were placed on different areas of the tongue. He found tiny differences—for example, the tip was *slightly* more sensitive to sweet than some other areas, and the back *slightly* more sensitive to bitter. But these differences were so minor that they were more like “a whisper louder” than an actual zoning. All areas could detect all tastes. 2. **The “telephone game” of science communication:** In the 1940s, a psychologist named Edwin Boring took Hanig’s raw data and turned it into a simplified graph for a textbook. In doing so, he exaggerated the minor sensitivity differences into what looked like strict zones—showing sweetness spiking only at the tip, bitterness only at the back, etc. The graph was visually compelling and easy to remember, so it spread like wildfire. 3. **Textbooks copy each other:** Textbook authors often rely on previous textbooks, especially for simplified foundational concepts. The tongue map became a tidy, memorable diagram that fit well on a page. It’s much harder to explain the nuanced truth: that every taste bud contains receptor cells for multiple (often all) taste qualities, that taste receptors are distributed all over the tongue, and that processing is complex. So the myth was repeated for generations, even as research in the 1970s and later utterly dismantled it. By the t